        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 4 Examining the GNOME Desktop\nP\u00e1ginas: 77-98\n\u2022 The History of GNOME  (77)\n\u2022 Breaking Down the GNOME Desktop  (78)\n  \u2022 Menus, please!  (79)\n  \u2022 The desktop  (83)\n\u2022 Exploring the Activities Overview  (85)\n  \u2022 The dash  (85)\n  \u2022 The windows overview area  (89)\n  \u2022 Working with workspaces  (90)\n\u2022 Customizing Your Ride  (91)\n  \u2022 Background  (92)\n  \u2022 Appearance  (92)\n  \u2022 Displays  (93)\n  \u2022 Mouse and touchpad  (95)\n\u2022 A Closer Look at Accessibility Features  (96)\nChapter 4\nExamining the GNOME\nDesktop\nS\nome people like to characterize Linux as being an operating system built by\nnerds, exclusively for nerds. This instills visions of having to type cryptic\ncommands to get anything done on your computer. However, that can\u2019t be\nfurther from the truth! Linux supports several graphical desktop environments\nthat provide some of the most user-friendly interfaces available for desktop\ncomputers.\nWhat can be a bit confusing, though, is that there\u2019s no one standard Linux graphi-\ncal desktop. Linux is full of choices, and nowhere is that more evident than in the\ngraphical desktop world. In Chapters 4, 5, and 6, I cover the most common Linux\ngraphical desktop environments used by the popular Linux distributions. This\nchapter starts out the discussion by examining the GNOME desktop.",
